yze.Rmd-18-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:19:## 🎯 Estimate Corrected MMs or AMCEs\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-20-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:21:In conjoint analysis, default MMs and AMCEs can be **biased** due to measurement error from intra-respondent variability.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-22-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-23-**projoint** corrects for this bias automatically.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-24-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:25:The following instructions apply to choice-level data. What if you have profile-level data?<sup><a href=\"faq.html#what-if-i-have-profile-level-data\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none;\">&#9432;</a></sup> Our <a href=\"faq.html\" target=\"_blank\">FAQ Page</a> has instructions to estimate and visualize profile-level QOIs.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-26-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-27----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-28-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-29-## 📦 Prepare Example Data\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-30-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-31-> **Outcome naming & order (important)**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-32-> \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-33-> - List `.outcomes` in the **order questions were asked**.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-34-> - If you have a repeated task, its outcome must be the **last element**.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-35-> - For base tasks (all but last), the function reads the **digits** in each name as the task id (e.g., `\"choice4\"`, `\"Q4\"`, `\"task04\"` → task 4).  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-36-> - The **repeated base task** is inferred from the **first base outcome’s digits**. The repeated outcome itself **need not** contain digits—only its position (last) matters.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-37-> - Outcome strings should end with your choice labels; by default we parse the **last character** and expect `\"A\"`/`\"B\"`. If your survey uses `\"1\"`/`\"2\"` (or other endings), set `.choice_labels` accordingly.\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-85-  .att_notchoose = \"att1\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-86-  .lev_notchoose = \"level1\"\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-87-)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-88-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-89-mm2 <- projoint(out1, qoi)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-90-print(mm2)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-91-summary(mm2)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-92-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-93-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-94-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-95----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-96-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:97:## 📉 Estimate AMCEs\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-98-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:99:<details><summary><b>Choice-Level AMCEs (Specific Level)</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-100-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-101-Suppose you want to quantify how the choice probability **changes** between the following profile pairs:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-102-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-103-- choosing a profile that includes \"40% of pre-tax income\" (`level3`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) versus one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for Housing Cost (`att1`); **and**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-104-- [baseline] choosing a profile that includes \"30% of pre-tax income\" (`level2`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) versus one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for Housing Cost (`att1`); \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-105-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-106-averaging over all combinations of the other attributes and across respondents. Then write the following code:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-107-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-108-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-109-qoi <- set_qoi(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-110-  .structure = \"choice_level\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-111-  .estimand = \"amce\",\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-159-wide_fig <- function() list(fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-160-tall_fig <- function() list(fig.width = 6, fig.height = 7)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-161-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-162-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-163-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-164-```{r, echo=FALSE}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-165-library(projoint)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-166-library(ggplot2)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-167-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-168-data(out1_arranged, package = \"projoint\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-169-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-170-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:171:## 🎨 Visualize MMs or AMCEs\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-172-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-173-The **projoint** package provides ready-to-publish plotting tools for conjoint analysis results.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-174-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-175-<span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><b>Note:</b></span> The current version of **projoint** supports plotting choice-level MMs only.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:176:Support for **choice-level AMCEs** will be available in future updates!\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-177-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-178----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-179-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-180-### ⚖️ Choice-Level Analysis\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-181-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-182-<details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-183-<summary><b>Estimate</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-184-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-185-- Specify your quantity of interest:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-186-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-187-qoi_mm <- set_qoi(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-188-  .structure = \"choice_level\", # default\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-8-  %\\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-9-  %\\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-10----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-11-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-12-```{r setup, include=FALSE}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-13-library(projoint)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-14-library(dplyr)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-15-library(ggplot2)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-16-library(patchwork)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-17-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-18-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-19-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:20:## What's the difference between \"profile-level\" and \"choice-level\" conjoint analysis??\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-21-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-22-<details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:23:<summary>Choice-level analysis is simpler, easier, and more powerful than profile-level analysis.</summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-24-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:25:Conjoint designs originated in market research and psychology where each respondent is asked to rate each of two different profiles (e.g., two products). Each of the two ratings provided separate information, and the two are analyzed as separate observations. Researchers with this <em>profile-level</em> design find it convenient to arrange their data with one profile per row, and thus twice as many rows as respondents.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-26-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:27:<p>Unfortunately, when social scientists adopted the conjoint survey design, they kept the same profile-level design but changed the outcome measure from separate ratings to a single choice between the two profiles (e.g., to reflect a voter choice between two candidates). In this situation, respondents asked to make one choice between the two profiles that are exactly dependent, as choosing one necessarily meant not choosing the other (e.g., in a two-candidate partisan election, one observation would be \"Democrat\" and the other would be \"not the Republican\"). Using this profile-level design with 2*n rows but only n independent observations requires the introduction of complicated statistical procedures to correct for the dependence induced solely by the researcher's decision to organize the data in this complicated way.</p>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-28-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-29-<p>We recommend the much simpler and more powerful <em>choice-level</em> design.  The idea is to arrange data at the level of the respondent's choice, so that each row in the data matrix includes information about one choice (and both profiles together, with n observations and n rows). Our <a href=\"https://gking.harvard.edu/conjointE\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"external-link\"><em>AJPS</em> article</a> clarifies this point, shows how this choice-level analysis vastly simplifies the notation, statistical analysis procedures, and intuition, and greatly expands the substantive questions conjoint analysis be used to answer.</p>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-30-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-31-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:32:## What if I have profile-level data?\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-33-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:34:<details><summary>Transform your data to choice-level, which is simpler and more powerful. To replicate old analyses, you can also use our profile-level tools</summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-35-  <details><summary><b>Profile-Level MMs (All Levels)</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-36-  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-37-```{r, echo = FALSE}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-38-data(\"exampleData1\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-39-outcomes <- paste0(\"choice\", 1:8)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-40-outcomes <- c(outcomes, \"choice1_repeated_flipped\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-41-out1 <- reshape_projoint(exampleData1, outcomes)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-42-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-43-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-44-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd:45:mm0 <- projoint(out1, .structure = \"profile_level\", .estimand = \"mm\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-46-print(mm0)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-47-summary(mm0)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-48-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-49-  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-50-  </details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-51-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-52-  <details><summary><b>Profile-Level MMs (Specific Level)</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-53-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.Rmd-54-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/faq.